window.PFT_APP_INFO={version:"2026.10.27.2",released:"2026-10-27",releaseNotes:["Fix mobile Cash Flow record actions with accessible Edit and Delete icons.","Add Obsidian Black in Appearance and remove Classic, migrating saved Classic choices to Modern.","Replace mobile Home Add record row with a small floating +; show borderless sync time at the page bottom beside Search.","Replace the duplicate Home debt tile with Monthly Expenses; hide pending Expense Snapshots and add a month selector for calculated snapshots.","Use one mobile Cash Flow month filter for both histories, summary and breakdown; remove duplicate visible history filters.","Fix mobile record overlap and clipped Edit/Delete menus; combine recurring settings with the commitments header.","Slim Cash Flow toggles, align recorded expenses and add SVG accordion chevrons.","Add shared glass filter popovers with keyboard controls, focus return and solid-background fallback.","Unify Investment and Retirement cards, empty states and Add menus; compact mobile portfolio metrics into one hero.","Simplify Debt overview and account counts, fix title/icon spacing, and match its Add button and empty state.","Use a transparent themed projection chart with area gradients and an interactive three-scenario breakdown.","Fix zero-value analytics charts, add a muted allocation ring, tint planned changes and make the FY table scrollable with a sticky first column.","Group Settings identity and sync in a profile card, move Sign out into Account, and use a distinct Settings navigation icon."],sections:[["Short first EMI interest","New monthly loans use actual-days / 365 for a first EMI earlier than the one-calendar-month anniversary (clamped in shorter months). Example: March 1 to March 5 is four elapsed days, so ₹10,00,000 at 12% accrues about ₹1,315 interest. At or after one full monthly cycle, one regular monthly interest amount applies; any extra days are estimated separately at principal × annual rate × extra days ÷ 36,500. For example, March 1 to April 5 is one monthly interest amount (₹10,000 at ₹10,00,000 and 12%) plus four extra days (₹1,315.07). The Extra days' interest with first EMI field fills automatically and can be edited to match the bank. The first suggested payment includes the extra amount so the normal EMI principal portion remains unchanged. A statement correction remains authoritative and does not double-charge this estimate. Existing loans preserve their saved first-EMI method until edited and confirmed. This is a first-payment estimate, not a full daily-accrual model; use Daily interest basis (SBI style) for daily balances and month-end capitalization."],["Revising the regular EMI","Open Debts, click a loan and choose Revise EMI. Enter the effective repayment date and revised regular monthly payment. Preview the EMI and estimated remaining number of payments, then confirm. Increasing the regular EMI reduces estimated tenure without changing rate or borrowed principal. For example, if a loan has ₹951315 outstanding at 12%, raising regular EMI from ₹50000 to ₹82000 changes the monthly-model estimate from 22 payments to 13. Final payment may be smaller. Previously recorded payment amounts are preserved; a revision affects the future regular amount and the schedule from its date. Linked recurring EMI entries quote the regular EMI applicable on each due date. An extra one-time payment uses Part Payment and its existing Keep EMI or Reduce EMI choices. A one-time higher payment does not silently become the future regular EMI. Revise EMI history can be edited or removed, with later repayments replayed and validated. Overlapping rate/EMI changes on one date must be combined through the existing terms-change entry."],["First EMI date and bank-confirmed extra interest","Enter the bank-confirmed First EMI date when the bank's first installment differs from the schedule. If only a monthly EMI day is entered, the app uses its existing minimum-30-day schedule. For monthly-interest loans, the Extra days' interest with first EMI box estimates interest for days beyond the first monthly anniversary and allows a manual bank-confirmed adjustment. If the first EMI is earlier than one full month, the full short period is already prorated and the extra-days box is zero. The first suggested EMI includes the estimated extra interest; it is treated as interest, so the regular EMI's principal portion remains unchanged. Leave the estimate as-is when it matches the bank, edit it when the bank confirms a different amount, or use the calculated estimate button to restore the estimate. Daily-balance loans calculate interest through the daily ledger and hide this monthly-only field. Statement corrections contain the full interest amount including any extra and are not charged twice. After monthly payments are recorded, use a statement correction to change their interest split."],["Monthly recurring entries","Open Income & Expense, then Recurring items to add monthly income, expenses or an EMI linked to an existing loan. Due entries are recorded while the app is open or when it is opened again; the app does not make bank payments. Earlier missed months stay in a review list. Select the months to add, adjust the amount for that month, or skip them. Day 31 is clamped to the last day in shorter months; day 0 means the last day. An optional end month stops future occurrences. Pause stops posting, and months during a pause are skipped when you resume. Edit future changes income/expense amounts from the next month; old records and older missed months retain their original schedule amounts. Edit the actual income or expense record to adjust only that month. A posted or skipped occurrence is remembered, so deleting a generated entry does not recreate it. Existing manual salary, matching expense and monthly EMI records are checked before adding another. For multiple expenses with identical category and amount, review the matched record. EMI schedules use the bank-confirmed first EMI date when entered; otherwise the existing minimum-30-day start rule applies. Use the loan's EMI due day. Earlier missed EMI months must be handled before later EMIs. EMI amount, interest, principal and remaining balance come from the loan repayment model, including dated rate changes, top-ups and part payments. The final EMI is capped at principal plus model interest, and posting stops at payoff. Recorded recurring expenses enter the existing cash-flow and residual house-expense calculation once, without an extra deduction."],["Adding dropdown options and account photos","Use + Add new in income source, expense category, debt type or investment type while entering a record. The form keeps its draft; the saved option is selected automatically. New investment types require a market-linked, fixed-return or no-return calculation. Add family member names in Settings > Family Access; adding a name does not grant account access. Settings > Account provides Add, Change and Remove photo for the signed-in account only. Photos are cropped to a square, compressed and privately stored separately from financial backups. They do not change your Google profile. The header photo still opens the family-profile selector. A saved device copy is shown offline; changing the photo requires an internet connection."],["Loan rate changes and top-ups","Open a loan and choose Update interest rate or Top up loan. Enter the effective repayment date or top-up date, and choose Keep remaining tenure, Keep current EMI, or Enter bank-confirmed EMI. The default calculates a revised EMI from the estimated remaining payoff time at the effective date, rounding the EMI up to a whole rupee. Keep current EMI recalculates the estimated remaining payments. Review the preview and confirm before saving. Rate arrows move by 0.05 percentage points. A new rate applies to the monthly EMI on or after that repayment date; earlier EMIs retain their previous rate. This uses monthly interest, not a daily split-rate bank calculation. A top-up adds principal from its date; the next EMI uses balance-day weighting for mid-cycle principal changes, as with part payments. Top-ups do not create cash-flow income or expense records. Record money received separately if required. Original principal is retained; total borrowed includes top-ups. Existing statement interest overrides are preserved. Preview recalculated outstanding, rate and EMI before saving. History entries can be edited or removed if later repayments remain valid. Only today or earlier dates are supported."],["1. Dashboard and family profiles","Use Dashboard for the current financial position and previous-month expense totals. The family profile selector filters member-owned income, investments, retirement accounts and debt. Household expenses and the available cash-balance calculation remain shared. A profile filter does not create a separate tracker or grant someone access; use Family Access for permissions."],["2. Income, expenses and monthly cash",`Record income and expenses with their actual dates. Month and financial-year filters let you review older entries. Annual expenses count in full in the month recorded; they are not spread over 12 months.
Available balance = current month starting balance + all household income - recorded expenses - PMS contributions. If no starting balance is recorded, the starting amount is treated as zero.
Example: INR 50,000 opening + INR 3,00,000 income - INR 80,000 expenses - INR 2,00,000 PMS contributions = INR 70,000 available. This is a recorded-flow estimate, not a bank feed.`],["3. House expense and full expense",`Enter each month opening bank/cash balance in Monthly Surplus History. The following month opening balance supplies the closing point for the previous month.
House expense = opening balance + income - recorded expenses - PMS contributions - next month opening balance.
Example: 50,000 + 3,00,000 - 80,000 - 2,00,000 - 40,000 = INR 30,000 house expense. Full expense = house expense + recorded expenses = INR 1,10,000.
The residual estimates spending not separately recorded. It stays Pending without the next balance. A negative residual means you should review balances or missing inflows. The dashboard names these tiles after the previous month. Do not enter the residual again as an expense unless correcting the underlying records.`],["4. Assets, debt and net worth",`Assets = current values of included investments + PF + PPF + NPS. Remaining loan principal is the debt total; future interest is not added to principal.
Net worth = assets - remaining debt. Example: INR 50,00,000 assets - INR 10,00,000 debt = INR 40,00,000. EPS is displayed separately from the PF current-value total. Cash-flow income is not automatically converted into a new investment holding. Archived accounts stay out of current totals.`],["5. Financial years, averages and ratios",`The financial year runs April to March. Current-year averages include records through the current month.
Average income = recorded income total / number of months with income entries. Expenses and contributions each use their own count of months with entries. A missing month is not automatically a zero-spend or zero-income month.
Example: income of INR 1,00,000 in April and INR 2,00,000 in June gives INR 1,50,000 average across two recorded months. Expense/income ratio = recorded expense total / recorded income total x 100; it is unavailable when income is zero. Contribution totals include retirement contributions (including EPS), investment contributions and PMS contributions.`],["6. Adding investments and recording changes","Add the investment under its category with the invested amount, current value, start date and applicable return assumptions. Contributions add capital; a current-value or profit/loss update changes valuation. Keep the dated contribution history accurate for XIRR. Fixed-return categories use their account rate; market growth assumptions affect projections, not a guaranteed actual return. Custom investment types can define their growth behavior in Custom Values."],["7. Gain, absolute return and CAGR",`Gain/loss = current value - invested amount. Absolute return = (current / invested - 1) x 100.
Example: INR 1,00,000 invested and INR 1,21,000 current gives INR 21,000 gain and 21% absolute return.
CAGR = ((current / invested)^(365 / elapsed days) - 1) x 100. Over exactly 730 days, the example gives 10% per year. CAGR treats capital as a lump sum and does not account for different contribution dates. Zero capital or an unsuitable time period can leave a return unavailable.`],["8. XIRR and dated contributions",`XIRR accounts for the timing of cash flows. Contributions are negative cash flows and the current value is a positive terminal cash flow on the valuation date. It solves: sum(cash flow / (1 + r)^(days since first flow / 365)) = 0; the displayed return is r x 100.
Example: invest INR 1,00,000 on 1 Jan 2025 and value it at INR 1,10,000 on 1 Jan 2026: XIRR is 10%. An additional mid-year contribution changes XIRR even if total invested is unchanged. The calculation needs both positive and negative cash flows and a solvable rate; otherwise it shows unavailable. NPS/PMS history and applicable market-investment histories supply these dates.`],["9. PF, PPF, NPS and EPS contributions",`Enable automatic monthly contributions only where needed. The schedule catches up missing eligible months when the app opens; it does not require the website to run in the background. Existing contribution periods prevent duplicate automatic entries.
PF addition = employee + employer - EPS. Example: INR 12,000 employee + INR 12,000 employer - INR 1,250 EPS = INR 22,750 added to PF, with INR 1,250 tracked as EPS. Disabling automatic contributions makes the scheduled amount zero. PPF can have no monthly contribution. Actual interest credits and account-value updates are recorded separately; future projected interest does not change actual balances.`],["10. Loan EMI and remaining principal",`Open a debt tile to see its details and repayment history. Record a Monthly EMI against the loan to split the payment into principal and interest.
Monthly rate = annual percentage rate / 1200. For a standard full EMI interval, interest = outstanding principal x monthly rate; principal paid = EMI - interest.
Example: INR 1,00,000 at 12% annually gives INR 1,000 interest for a normal monthly interval. A INR 5,000 EMI pays INR 4,000 principal, leaving INR 96,000. The loan reduces only by principal, while the full EMI is an expense. An interest override from a statement takes precedence. Between EMI dates, balances are weighted by days within that EMI interval; this is not an actual/365 daily-interest model. Late fees are not added automatically.`],["11. Part payments and estimated remaining EMIs",`Record Part Payment for a principal-only reduction: the amount paid reduces principal and interest is zero for that payment entry. Choose Keep EMI to estimate a shorter repayment term, or Reduce EMI to recalculate payment over the estimated remaining term.
EMI = P x r / (1 - (1 + r)^(-n)), where P is remaining principal, r is the monthly rate and n is the estimated remaining months. At zero interest EMI = P / n. The app rounds recalculated EMI to whole rupees.
Remaining EMIs are simulated from the outstanding balance, rate and EMI. They are estimates; future part payments, lender rounding, interest overrides or rate changes can change the outcome. An EMI that cannot cover interest cannot amortize normally.`],["12. Growth projection: normal assets",`Use Growth Projection to set assumptions and planned contributions, additions or withdrawals. Projections begin next month, starting from the current recorded values.
Monthly growth factor = (1 + annual return / 100)^(1/12). Projected month close = (opening value + contributions + planned additions - withdrawals) x monthly factor, subject to the account settings.
Example: at 12% annual growth, the monthly factor is about 1.009489. INR 1,00,000 opening plus INR 10,000 contributed at the beginning of the month projects to about INR 1,11,043.77. Scheduled contribution step-ups follow financial-year settings. These calculations describe assumptions, not promised market returns.`],["13. Growth projection: PF, PPF and EPS",`PF and PPF use a different projection method: monthly interest = eligible pre-return balance x annual rate / 1200. Interest accumulates and is credited in March rather than compounding into the balance every month.
Example: INR 1,00,000 at 8.15% earns about INR 679.17 for one projected month before later contributions or withdrawals alter the base. EPS has zero projected return and no contribution step-up in this model. Projection inclusion toggles control the included retirement assets; excluding PF also excludes its EPS projection. Actual account statements and posted interest may differ from these simplified scenarios.`],["14. Analytics, reports and custom values","Analytics summarizes recorded values, allocation, returns and trends for the chosen profile and period. Reports let you review relevant financial years and date ranges. Category and member lists are managed in Settings > Custom Values. Values already used by records cannot be deleted until the references are addressed. Changing an assumption affects projections; it does not rewrite historical cash-flow entries."],["15. Data, backup and Records & checks",`In Settings > Data & backup, export JSON for an independent copy or import a validated backup. Recovery snapshot lets you inspect the available recovery copy. Expand Records & checks for statement comparisons, archived accounts and the local change log.
A statement check records a comparison; it does not change the balance by itself. Correct the account value or EMI split in the relevant controls when needed. Archived accounts retain history but stop contributing to current totals and projections. Restoring resumes contributions from next month without filling archived months. Change log is device-local rather than a shared audit trail.
Delete Dashboard in Settings > Account is owner-only. It shows the affected owner email, offers Download backup, requires typing DELETE DASHBOARD, an online connection and no pending changes. The cloud clear must succeed before the local copy is cleared; a failed offline attempt is not queued for later. The login and family-access permissions remain.`],["16. Family Access and permissions",`Choose the tracker in Your family tracker. Owners invite a verified Google email as Viewer or Editor; the member accepts inside the app. Viewer can read and export. Editor can add, edit and delete financial records. Owner manages invitations and bulk import, recovery and clear actions. Change roles or revoke access in Family members with access.
Opening a shared tracker requires an online permission check. Once open, an editor can save offline and sync after reconnecting. Previously downloaded copies cannot be recalled. The profile selector and access permissions are separate: choosing a family profile is a viewing filter, not a security boundary.`],["17. Cloud status, refreshing and offline use",`Changes save on the device and queue for cloud sync while the app is open. Last cloud update shows the most recent successful cloud snapshot time, displayed in this device local time; a pending or failed save does not advance it. If no timestamp is known, the app says it is not available yet.
Refresh cloud records reloads the latest cloud snapshot and preserves the selected page. A browser refresh also returns to the selected page for this signed-in tracker. Pending changes require review before being replaced. Concurrent changes are detected by revision; there is no automatic merge of financial edits. Export a backup before resolving a conflict. App & updates shows the installed version, online latest-version check and expandable release notes.
Cloud status and the last successful cloud update are shown in Settings. Review differences is available there when another device has newer changes. A compact sync status and last successful sync time appear only when the page bottom is reached, beside Search. Detailed controls remain in Settings.`],["18. Account and deletion","Account shows the currently signed-in Google email. Delete Dashboard clears your own tracker records, custom values, recovery snapshot and local change log from the cloud and this device. It keeps your app login, Google account and family access. Other people’s trackers and downloaded backups are unchanged. Only the owner can use it, while viewing their own tracker. Download a backup, finish syncing and type DELETE DASHBOARD to confirm. You must be online; the cloud clear must succeed before local records are cleared. This uses the regular cloud sync and does not require the account-deletion service."],["19. Reviewing changes and calculation details",`Before importing a backup, review the current/backup record counts and records that would be added, changed or removed. Import still replaces current records; it does not merge them. Older files may lack an export date or tracker identity. You can download the current records before confirming. New exports include the date, tracker identity and app version.
In App info, expand Calculation breakdowns using your records to see the existing amounts behind Net Worth, Available Balance, House Expense and Full Expense. The family profile applies to personal assets and debts; household cash and expenses remain shared.
If another device has newer cloud records, Review differences compares the two copies without changing either one or resolving the conflict automatically. Export the device backup before using the existing cloud refresh. History is saved on the current device and now includes the signed-in session for new entries; imported actor details are informational. On mobile, Quick Add opens the existing entry forms. No calculation or contribution schedule is changed by these review tools.`],["20. Page styles and appearance","Settings > Appearance > Page style offers Modern (default) and Emerald Flow. Obsidian Black is available as a color palette. Saved Classic preferences switch to Modern. Modern uses top navigation on desktop and six main tabs on mobile. Analytics & Reports includes Analytics, Growth Projection and Date range reports. Both use the same records, calculations, forms, cloud sync and permissions. The selected style is saved per signed-in tracker context on this device. It is independent of light/dark mode and colour selection. Switching styles keeps the current page and any unfinished form entries."],["Complete backup and payment review","Export a current complete backup from Settings to include financial records, shared review marks, valuation dates, planner inputs and payment confirmations. Import previews replacement before saving. Records and reviews publish together; offline imports stay saved on this device until syncing succeeds. Resolve newer shared reviews from Sync details. Unversioned financial-only or obsolete backup formats are not imported. Current device-recovery files preserve existing shared reviews when a review snapshot was unavailable. Use the Search button at the bottom of the screen from any page. In Monthly close, Edit amount opens the contribution editor; Save & return to review saves the correction and returns to the statement check. Payment confirmation never posts a contribution again."]]},window.downloadTrackerGuide=async function(){window.jspdf?.jsPDF||await window.PFTLoadScript("pdf");const info=window.PFT_APP_INFO,pdf=new window.jspdf.jsPDF({unit:"mm",format:"a4"});let y=24;const width=174,bottom=274;function pageRoom(height){y+height>bottom&&(pdf.addPage(),y=24)}pdf.setTextColor(15,75,77),pdf.setFont("helvetica","bold"),pdf.setFontSize(22),pdf.text("Personal Finance Tracker",18,y),y+=10,pdf.setFontSize(15),pdf.text("App info",18,y),y+=9,pdf.setFont("helvetica","normal"),pdf.setFontSize(10),pdf.setTextColor(90),pdf.text("Version "+info.version+" | Released "+info.released,18,y),y+=13;for(const[title,body]of info.sections){pdf.setFont("helvetica","normal"),pdf.setFontSize(10);const lines=pdf.splitTextToSize(body,width);pageRoom(Math.min(250,lines.length*5+16)),pdf.setTextColor(15,75,77),pdf.setFont("helvetica","bold"),pdf.setFontSize(12),pdf.text(title,18,y),y+=8,pdf.setFont("helvetica","normal"),pdf.setFontSize(10),pdf.setTextColor(35);for(const line of lines)pageRoom(5),pdf.text(line,18,y),y+=5;y+=8}const count=pdf.getNumberOfPages();for(let n=1;n<=count;n++)pdf.setPage(n),pdf.setDrawColor(210),pdf.line(18,282,192,282),pdf.setFontSize(9),pdf.setTextColor(100),pdf.text("App info | "+info.version,18,288),pdf.text(n+" / "+count,192,288,{align:"right"});pdf.save("Personal_Finance_Tracker_App_Guide.pdf")};

